To find the degree of the field extension \([ \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta) : \mathbb{Q} ]\), where \(\eta = (e^{\frac{\pi}{3}i})^2\), we start by noting that \(\eta\) is a primitive 3rd root of unity. Specifically, \(\eta = e^{2\pi i /3}\), which has minimal polynomial \(x^2 + x + 1\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\). Therefore, \([\mathbb{Q}(\eta) : \mathbb{Q}] = 2\).

Next, we consider the field extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta)\). We can use the tower law to compute the degree:
\[
[\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta) : \mathbb{Q}] = [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta) : \mathbb{Q}(\eta)] \cdot [\mathbb{Q}(\eta) : \mathbb{Q}]
\]

We need to determine \([\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta) : \mathbb{Q}(\eta)]\). The element \(\sqrt[3]{3}\) is a root of the polynomial \(x^3 - 3\). We check if this polynomial remains irreducible over \(\mathbb{Q}(\eta)\). If it is, then \([\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta) : \mathbb{Q}(\eta)] = 3\).

To check irreducibility, suppose \(x^3 - 3\) factors over \(\mathbb{Q}(\eta)\). It would then have a root in \(\mathbb{Q}(\eta)\). Suppose \(\alpha = a + b\eta\) (with \(a, b \in \mathbb{Q}\)) is a root. Calculating \((a + b\eta)^3\) and equating to 3 leads to equations showing no such \(a, b\) exist in \(\mathbb{Q}\). Thus, \(x^3 - 3\) is irreducible over \(\mathbb{Q}(\eta)\), giving \([\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta) : \mathbb{Q}(\eta)] = 3\).

Since \([\mathbb{Q}(\eta) : \mathbb{Q}] = 2\), the total degree is:
\[
[\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{3}, \eta) : \mathbb{Q}] = 3 \cdot 2 = 6
\]

Thus, the degree of the field extension is \(\boxed{6}\).<|im_end|>
