The given recurrence relation is:

\[
T(n) = 3^k \cdot T\left(\dfrac{n}{2^k}\right) + c \cdot n \cdot \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} 3^i
\]

where \( c \) is a constant, \( T(1) = m \), and \( k = \log_2 n \).

First, substitute \( k = \log_2 n \):

- The term \( \dfrac{n}{2^k} = \dfrac{n}{2^{\log_2 n}} = \dfrac{n}{n} = 1 \), so \( T\left(\dfrac{n}{2^k}\right) = T(1) = m \).
- The sum \( \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} 3^i = \dfrac{3^k - 1}{2} \).

Substituting these into the recurrence:

\[
T(n) = 3^k \cdot m + c \cdot n \cdot \dfrac{3^k - 1}{2}
\]

Simplify the expression:

\[
T(n) = m \cdot 3^k + \dfrac{c \cdot n \cdot (3^k - 1)}{2}
\]

Recognize that \( 3^k = 3^{\log_2 n} = n^{\log_2 3} \). Therefore:

\[
T(n) = m \cdot n^{\log_2 3} + \dfrac{c \cdot n \cdot (n^{\log_2 3} - 1)}{2}
\]

The dominant term as \( n \) grows is \( \dfrac{c}{2} \cdot n \cdot n^{\log_2 3} = \dfrac{c}{2} \cdot n^{1 + \log_2 3} \). Thus, the time complexity is:

\[
T(n) = O\left(n^{1 + \log_2 3}\right)
\]

Equivalently, since \( 1 + \log_2 3 = \log_2 6 \), this can also be written as \( O(n^{\log_2 6}) \).

The final answer is:

\[
\boxed{O\left(n^{1 + \log_2 3}\right)}
\]<|im_end|>
