The limit is found by analyzing the pointwise behavior of the integrand. For \(x \in (0, \pi/4)\), \(\tan(x) < 1\), so \(\tan^n(x) \to 0\) and \(\frac{1}{1+\tan^n(x)} \to 1\). For \(x \in (\pi/4, \pi/3]\), \(\tan(x) > 1\), so \(\tan^n(x) \to \infty\) and \(\frac{1}{1+\tan^n(x)} \to 0\). The values at \(x=0\) and \(x=\pi/4\) are trivial. The pointwise limit is thus

\[
f(x) = 
\begin{cases}
1, & x \in (0, \pi/4), \\
0, & x \in (\pi/4, \pi/3].
\end{cases}
\]

Since \(0 \le \frac{1}{1+\tan^n(x)} \le 1\) for all \(n\) and the interval is finite, the Dominated Convergence Theorem applies. Therefore,

\[
\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_{0}^{\pi/3} \frac{1}{1+\tan^n(x)}\,dx = \int_{0}^{\pi/4} 1\,dx + \int_{\pi/4}^{\pi/3} 0\,dx = \frac{\pi}{4}.
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}
\]<|im_end|>
