The function simplifies to
\[
g(x)=\frac{2}{2+4x^2}=\frac{1}{1+2x^2}.
\]
Since \(x^2 \ge 0\) for all real \(x\), the denominator satisfies \(1+2x^2 \ge 1\), and therefore
\[
0 < g(x) \le 1.
\]
Equality \(g(x)=1\) occurs at \(x=0\). As \(|x|\to\infty\), \(g(x)\to 0\) but never actually equals \(0\). Hence the range of \(g\) is the interval \((0,1]\).

Here \(a=0\) and \(b=1\), so \(a+b = 0+1 = 1\).

